<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lillibullero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">tune first published <a href="/years/1686">1686</a>; Wharton's words <a href="/years/1686">1686</a>; broadsides and wide popularity <a href="/years/1688">1688</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The melody of &quot;Lillibullero&quot; is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-purcell">Henry Purcell</a>, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> survives.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…European congregations kept two older tunes for parts of the year: one for the Omer weeks between Passover and Shavuot, which has been compared to &quot;<b>Lillibullero</b>&quot; and to Mozart's Marriage of Figaro and resembles late 17th-century German folk song, and one for the Three Weeks before Tisha b'Av.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lekha-dodi">Lekha Dodi</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody of &quot;<b>Lillibullero</b>&quot; is attributed to Henry Purcell, though it is unclear whether he composed it or arranged it; it was first published in The Delightful Companion, John Playford's method book for the recorder, in 1686, and no original manuscript survives.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lillibullero">Lillibullero</a></li></ul></section>
